<div class="bg-zinc-950 text-zinc-100">
  <.marketing_nav current_user={@current_user} current={:docs} />

  <section class="py-12 sm:py-16">
    <.docs_layout
      current="autoscaling-fleets"
      updated="September 3, 2026"
      toc={[
        {"model", "How it works"},
        {"key", "Create a reusable enrollment key"},
        {"boot", "The boot install"},
        {"clouds", "GCP, AWS, and Azure"},
        {"identity", "A new runner each boot"},
        {"cleanup", "Reaping terminated instances"},
        {"next", "After it is connected"}
      ]}
    >
      <.docs_header current="autoscaling-fleets">
        <:dek>
          An autoscaling group — a GCP managed instance group, an AWS Auto Scaling group, an
          Azure Scale Set — creates and terminates hosts on its own. Give the launch template one
          reusable enrollment key, and each instance enrolls a runner during boot with no per-host
          key or approval.
        </:dek>
      </.docs_header>

      <p class="mt-6 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        For hosts that stay put, see <.link
          navigate={~p"/docs/host-install"}
          class="text-brand-400 hover:text-brand-300"
        >Linux host</.link>.
      </p>

      <.docs_prerequisites>
        <:item>
          An autoscaling group on GCP, AWS, or Azure — or anything else that boots instances from
          a launch template.
        </:item>
        <:item>A secret manager the boot script can read the enrollment key from.</:item>
        <:item>
          A launch image with GitHub CLI support for <.docs_inline_code>gh attestation verify --bundle</.docs_inline_code>.
        </:item>
        <:item>
          Outbound HTTPS from every instance to <code class="text-zinc-300">emisar.dev:443</code>
          and <code class="text-zinc-300">registry.emisar.dev:443</code>. See
          <.link
            navigate={~p"/docs/network-requirements"}
            class="text-brand-400 hover:text-brand-300"
          >Network requirements</.link>
          for the required public trust-root hosts and optional GitHub fallback domains.
        </:item>
      </.docs_prerequisites>

      <.docs_h2 id="model">How it works</.docs_h2>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        A host install uses a new single-use key for one machine; a fleet uses one
        <strong class="text-zinc-200">reusable</strong>
        key, because every instance starts from the same template. On boot each instance runs the
        same <.link
          href={~p"/install.sh"}
          class="text-brand-400 hover:text-brand-300"
        >installer</.link>, which downloads the runner, exchanges the key for the runner's own token,
        and connects. Holding a valid key is the whole enrollment check — no one approves each
        host.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        Set
        <.docs_inline_code>EMISAR_GROUP</.docs_inline_code>
        in the boot
        script to set the instance's fleet tier. Runbooks and runner scopes then target the fleet
        instead of one host.
      </p>

      <.docs_h2 id="key">Create a reusable enrollment key</.docs_h2>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        In the console, open <a
          href={~p"/app/runners/keys/new"}
          class="font-medium text-brand-300 underline decoration-brand-500/30 underline-offset-4 hover:text-brand-200"
        >Runners → Enrollment keys → New key</a>. Turn on <strong>Reusable</strong>
        so every instance can enroll with the same one. Two optional limits keep a shared key honest:
      </p>
      <ul class="mt-4 space-y-3 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        <li class="flex items-start gap-2.5">
          <span class="flex-none text-zinc-500">—</span>
          <span>
            <strong class="text-zinc-200">Max uses.</strong>
            Cap the total number of runners the key can ever enroll — a ceiling that survives a leak.
            Uses are never refunded when an instance terminates, so budget for every boot over the
            key's life, not the group's peak size.
          </span>
        </li>
        <li class="flex items-start gap-2.5">
          <span class="flex-none text-zinc-500">—</span>
          <span>
            <strong class="text-zinc-200">Expiry.</strong>
            Set the date when the key stops enrolling runners, so a forgotten template cannot add
            runners forever.
          </span>
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        The
        <.docs_inline_code>emkey-enroll-…</.docs_inline_code>
        secret appears once. Store it in the secret manager, and let the boot script read it at
        start. Never bake the key into an image.
      </p>
      <.docs_callout kind={:warn} title="A reusable key is a fleet-wide credential">
        <p>
          Anyone holding this key can enroll a runner into your account — the limits above and
          account policy still apply, but treat it like the credential it is. Rotating and
          revoking it, and what each step reaches, is covered in <.link
            navigate={~p"/docs/runner-credentials"}
            class="text-brand-400 hover:text-brand-300"
          >Runner credentials</.link>.
        </p>
      </.docs_callout>

      <.docs_h2 id="boot">The boot install</.docs_h2>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        The boot script runs the same installer as a normal host install, plus two flags:
        <.docs_inline_code>--yes</.docs_inline_code>
        and <.docs_inline_code>--packs</.docs_inline_code>. Without both, the installer stops to ask questions no one is
        there to answer.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        Keep the enrollment key off the command line. Every user on the host can read any
        process's command line; only the process owner can read its environment. So the script
        exports the key first, then uses
        <.docs_inline_code>sudo -E</.docs_inline_code>
        to keep it in the environment through <.docs_inline_code>sudo</.docs_inline_code>:
      </p>
      <.docs_code
        phx-no-format
        label="emisar-boot.sh"
        copy_text="gh attestation verify --help 2>&amp;1 | grep -q -- '--bundle' || { echo 'GitHub CLI with attestation bundle verification is required' &gt;&amp;2; exit 1; }
export EMISAR_ENROLLMENT_KEY=&quot;$(your-secret-tool read emisar/enrollment-key)&quot;
curl -fsSL https://emisar.dev/install.sh | sudo -E env EMISAR_GROUP=web bash -s -- --yes --packs linux-core"
      ><span class="text-zinc-500">#!/usr/bin/env bash</span>
gh attestation verify --help 2&gt;&amp;1 | grep -q -- '--bundle' || &#123;
  echo 'GitHub CLI with attestation bundle verification is required' &gt;&amp;2
  exit 1
&#125;

<span class="text-zinc-500"># the key reaches the environment, never argv</span>
export EMISAR_ENROLLMENT_KEY="<span class="text-brand-300">$(your-secret-tool read emisar/enrollment-key)</span>"

curl -fsSL https://emisar.dev/install.sh \
  | sudo -E env EMISAR_GROUP=<span class="text-brand-300">web</span> \
      bash -s -- --yes --packs linux-core</.docs_code>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        The command line then does not carry the secret, and only root can read the key. The
        example reads it from your secret manager at boot because cloud user data is not a safe
        place for a secret.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        The pieces of that command:
      </p>
      <ul class="mt-4 space-y-3 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        <li class="flex items-start gap-2.5">
          <span class="flex-none text-zinc-500">—</span>
          <span><.docs_inline_code>--yes</.docs_inline_code>
          skips the prompts.</span>
        </li>
        <li class="flex items-start gap-2.5">
          <span class="flex-none text-zinc-500">—</span>
          <span>
            <.docs_inline_code>--packs</.docs_inline_code>
            lists the packs to install, and is required with <.docs_inline_code>--yes</.docs_inline_code>. An empty
            value installs none; you can add packs later.
          </span>
        </li>
        <li class="flex items-start gap-2.5">
          <span class="flex-none text-zinc-500">—</span>
          <span><.docs_inline_code>EMISAR_GROUP</.docs_inline_code>
          sets which fleet the runner joins.</span>
        </li>
        <li class="flex items-start gap-2.5">
          <span class="flex-none text-zinc-500">—</span>
          <span>
            <.docs_inline_code>EMISAR_RUNNER_LABEL_*</.docs_inline_code>
            variables add free-form labels — <.docs_inline_code>EMISAR_RUNNER_LABEL_ROLE=web</.docs_inline_code>.
          </span>
        </li>
        <li class="flex items-start gap-2.5">
          <span class="flex-none text-zinc-500">—</span>
          <span>
            <.docs_inline_code>EMISAR_RUNNER_ID</.docs_inline_code>
            sets the name and identity the runner registers under, when the image's own
            hostnames are generated or repeat.
          </span>
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        The
        <.link
          navigate={~p"/docs/host-install"}
          class="text-brand-400 hover:text-brand-300"
        >Linux host</.link>
        page covers every other installer flag and config setting.
      </p>

      <.docs_h2 id="clouds">GCP, AWS, and Azure</.docs_h2>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        The install line is identical on every cloud. All that changes is where the launch template
        runs a boot script — save the command above as
        <.docs_inline_code>emisar-boot.sh</.docs_inline_code>
        and wire it in:
      </p>
      <ul class="mt-4 space-y-3 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        <li class="flex items-start gap-2.5">
          <span class="flex-none text-zinc-500">—</span>
          <span>
            <strong class="text-zinc-200">GCP Managed Instance Group.</strong>
            Add it to the instance template as startup-script metadata — <.docs_inline_code>gcloud compute instance-templates create … --metadata-from-file=startup-script=emisar-boot.sh</.docs_inline_code>.
          </span>
        </li>
        <li class="flex items-start gap-2.5">
          <span class="flex-none text-zinc-500">—</span>
          <span>
            <strong class="text-zinc-200">AWS Auto Scaling group.</strong>
            Put it in the launch template's user data (base64-encoded) — the
            <.docs_inline_code>UserData</.docs_inline_code>
            field of <.docs_inline_code>aws ec2 create-launch-template --launch-template-data</.docs_inline_code>.
          </span>
        </li>
        <li class="flex items-start gap-2.5">
          <span class="flex-none text-zinc-500">—</span>
          <span>
            <strong class="text-zinc-200">Azure Virtual Machine Scale Set.</strong>
            Pass it as cloud-init custom data — <.docs_inline_code>az vmss create … --custom-data emisar-boot.sh</.docs_inline_code>.
          </span>
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        Each cloud runs the script as root during first boot; the runner enrolls and appears in
        the fleet within seconds. For faster boots, bake the binary into a custom image with
        <.docs_inline_code>--no-start</.docs_inline_code>
        and let the boot script start and enroll it.
      </p>

      <.docs_h2 id="identity">A new runner each boot</.docs_h2>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        A runner uses its hostname as its identity unless you set <.docs_inline_code>runner.id</.docs_inline_code>. A new scale-out
        instance receives a generated hostname and registers as a new runner. A reboot keeps the
        hostname and reconnects the same runner, so scaling out by ten creates ten runners — the
        expected shape of an ephemeral fleet. Two rules follow:
      </p>
      <ul class="mt-4 space-y-3 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        <li class="flex items-start gap-2.5">
          <span class="flex-none text-zinc-500">—</span>
          <span>
            <strong class="text-zinc-200">Never bake a credential into an image.</strong>
            At first connect, the runner stores its token in <.docs_inline_code>/var/lib/emisar</.docs_inline_code>. A snapshot
            taken after enrollment copies one runner token into every instance built from that
            image. Build the image with <.docs_inline_code>--no-start</.docs_inline_code>. Let each instance
            enroll during its own boot.
          </span>
        </li>
        <li class="flex items-start gap-2.5">
          <span class="flex-none text-zinc-500">—</span>
          <span>
            <strong class="text-zinc-200">Use a stable runner ID when the hostname is unsuitable.</strong>
            If the group recycles hostnames, set
            <.docs_inline_code>runner.id</.docs_inline_code>
            from the instance
            metadata in the boot script. A cloud instance ID is a natural unique runner ID.
          </span>
        </li>
      </ul>

      <.docs_h2 id="cleanup">Reaping terminated instances</.docs_h2>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        A terminated instance does not remove its runner — the record just goes <em>offline</em>
        and stays in the fleet, where each dead host counts against the plan runner limit until
        removal.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        Turn on retention to remove them. On <a
          href={~p"/app/runners"}
          class="font-medium text-brand-300 underline decoration-brand-500/30 underline-offset-4 hover:text-brand-200"
        >Runners</a>,
        an owner or admin sets <strong>Remove runners inactive for</strong>, and an hourly sweep
        removes runners past the window — never a connected or disabled one. It is off by default.
        Pick a window longer than an expected disconnect, a rolling deployment or a zone outage,
        but short enough to clear dead hosts promptly. A removed host that returns enrolls as a
        new runner.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        Between sweeps, a high-churn group can still carry dead runners against its plan limit —
        trim them sooner with <strong>Clean up now</strong>
        on the same page. Removal, by sweep or by hand, keeps the runner's audit history.
      </p>
      <.docs_h2 id="next">After it is connected</.docs_h2>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        <.link navigate={~p"/docs/runner-fleet"} class="text-brand-400 hover:text-brand-300">
          Manage the runner fleet
        </.link>
        covers work after the first connection — groups, labels, pack credentials, updates,
        reconnects, and removal. To take a fleet to production in phases, see the <.link
          navigate={~p"/docs/production"}
          class="text-brand-400 hover:text-brand-300"
        >Go to production</.link>.
      </p>
    </.docs_layout>
  </section>

  <.marketing_footer />
</div>
